   "source": [
    "# Final Interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "This project successfully completed a detailed analysis of drinking water contaminants and a separate spatial analysis of surface water data in Kansas.\n",
    "\n",
    "From the drinking water analysis, most contaminants such as Barium, Fluoride, Selenium, and Chromium remain relatively stable over time with only minor variations. In contrast, nitrate shows significantly higher concentration levels and noticeable variation across years. The nitrate trend increases from approximately 2800 ppb in 2019 to nearly 5000 ppb in 2024, making it the most prominent contaminant in terms of concentration.\n",
    "\n",
    "The regression analysis showed that year is not a meaningful predictor when all contaminants are combined, with an R-squared value close to zero and a high p-value. This indicates that contaminant behavior varies individually, and aggregated modeling does not capture meaningful patterns.\n",
    "\n",
    "From the surface water analysis, the dataset provided usable geographic information, allowing visualization of monitoring locations across Kansas. The spatial distribution shows that monitoring sites are spread across the state, although not uniformly.\n",
    "\n",
    "The nitrate-specific spatial analysis shows that contamination is distributed across multiple locations rather than concentrated in a single hotspot. Additionally, the surface water nitrate trend fluctuates over time, with values varying between approximately 0.8 and 1.7, indicating no consistent linear trend.\n",
    "\n",
    "The site-level nitrate summary further shows that some monitoring locations have higher average nitrate values than others, but these higher values are not concentrated in one specific region. This supports the conclusion that nitrate contamination is spatially distributed rather than localized.\n",
    "\n",
    "A key limitation of this project is the inability to directly link surface water data with drinking water systems. The drinking water dataset does not contain latitude, longitude, or a shared identifier, which prevents spatial joining of the two datasets. As a result, the findings should be interpreted as two connected analyses rather than a direct cause-and-effect relationship.\n",
    "\n",
    "Overall, this project highlights the importance of contaminant-specific analysis, data cleaning, and spatial context when working with environmental datasets. Future work could focus on integrating watershed-level data or geographic identifiers to better understand the relationship between surface water and drinking water systems."
   ]
  }
